# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Template. Copy to `.env` and fill in real values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This file is committed, so it must never hold a real
# credential. Wrangler reads `.env` from the project root automatically, so
# `pnpm cf:deploy` needs no flags once these are set.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Account ID — Cloudflare dashboard → Workers & Pages → right-hand sidebar.
# Not a secret, but Wrangler needs it to know which account to deploy into.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# API token — dashboard → My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token.
# Easiest: use the "Edit Cloudflare Workers" template, scoped to this account.
#
# If you build a custom token instead, grant these Account permissions:
#   Workers Scripts    Edit   — upload the Worker and its static assets
#   Workers Scripts    Read   — grant alongside Edit; Edit-only tokens fail
#                               with a confusing authorization error
#   Account Settings   Read   — let Wrangler resolve the account
#   D1                 Edit   — REQUIRED since the enquiry database landed.
#                               Without it, `wrangler deploy` fails with
#                               "D1 binding 'DB' references database ... which
#                               was not found" — which reads like a wrong ID
#                               but is really a permissions error. The database
#                               is invisible to the token, so it reports it as
#                               missing.
#
# No Zone permissions are needed to publish to *.workers.dev. Add
# "Workers Routes: Edit" only once you attach a custom domain.
#
# This IS a secret. Treat it like a password; rotate it if it ever leaks.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Enquiry notifications are sent with Resend, and its API key does NOT belong
# in this file. The Worker cannot read .env at runtime — it reads a Wrangler
# secret, set once:
#
#     npx wrangler secret put RESEND_API_KEY
#
# Scope the key to sending from pixelkriti.com only. The domain must be
# verified in Resend (DKIM + SPF records) or every send is rejected.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET were here for a Gmail-API approach
# that was built and then dropped: Gmail sends as the authenticated mailbox
# (so clients would get mail from a gmail.com address), and its refresh token
# expires after 7 days unless the OAuth consent screen is published — failing
# silently when it does. If those keys are unused elsewhere, revoke them in
# Google Cloud Console rather than leaving live credentials lying about.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything prefixed with VITE_ is INLINED INTO THE PUBLIC JS BUNDLE and served
# to every visitor. Never prefix a credential with VITE_.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
